#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092012 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092013 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5458" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "7091b406-96d1-5663-9575-66f26c6a6267" :question/math-academy-id "e-7186" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbc60a6d-2862-52aa-b8d3-7f5efd1a1738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302241} {:answer/id #uuid "1c46cf2d-9bff-5f3b-8ecf-f5bd8754777f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302242} {:answer/id #uuid "9506c4ca-6a11-5c3d-b959-ee562bbba4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302243} {:answer/id #uuid "33c35414-c1a7-5d57-9d69-e73a67690ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302244} {:answer/id #uuid "52109998-cd20-53b1-a3e0-e455f318a62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbc60a6d-2862-52aa-b8d3-7f5efd1a1738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302241} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd2d3ff-916a-554f-b5ff-99fc73d2ad5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bc23a19-df35-5757-b9a8-0b3bf67f1e4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11961" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3),(0,5),(2,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-2)$\n- $(-5,0)$\n- $(5,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f07cecb-b658-5b3b-8cba-fc8183b56089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089227} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3ccb11-9bde-5497-aa8c-93ce9414643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089228} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7b5b87-0e72-5959-b794-ed035f8ec46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089229} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d6f2b1-b504-523d-9650-7659b2e6ec45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089230} {:answer/id #uuid "19979801-1f64-5840-b1ce-438e2af067dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f3ccb11-9bde-5497-aa8c-93ce9414643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089228} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc24ddc-ff1f-59d3-a31e-b340bb39ac85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac0c7623-4587-52d5-8de6-ad1d378c8244" :question/math-academy-id "q-12121" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-2),(-1,2),(1,1).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(1,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$\n- $(-1,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,2),(1,-2),(-1,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911977f9-4dd7-5952-a7d2-5e7840939b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286104} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb95726-da34-5373-afcd-2fe81b267f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286105} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d4bc3d-a55e-5bbb-8cf3-be60cc08dce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286106} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7d6c0b-89d4-58f6-b838-87a105cabcb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286107} {:answer/id #uuid "1595331b-914e-55d1-ad79-8a6281f2e79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "911977f9-4dd7-5952-a7d2-5e7840939b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286104} :answer-field/id #uuid "7765a4c1-9acf-5c4a-b959-40fe56dae2b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18446b37-20cc-5e44-a427-24676c932277" :question/math-academy-id "q-12124" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-1),(1,3),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-3,1)$\n- $(-3,2)$\n- $(1,-2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-3,1),(3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7b110b-04b2-5dff-9776-a58956cadb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283664} {:answer/id #uuid "40dd50a4-b29a-5f50-bc4f-56a7c986dd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283665} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5120bc-03c3-561a-9972-81c26fc7d824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283666} {:answer/id #uuid "ef45b474-23d4-5db1-b303-9eba3be7ca1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283667} {:answer/id #uuid "a282c8ad-012f-5852-9cf1-fb7fb0ba2903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a282c8ad-012f-5852-9cf1-fb7fb0ba2903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283668} :answer-field/id #uuid "52390b55-2534-5dcd-a08a-0142a01ed0c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0af2085-c5e5-5d4f-bf4d-73318bbad63b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160371" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(6,-1),(-4,1),(3,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-6,-1)$\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(3,-5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,1),(4,-1),(-3,-5).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3697d24e-db87-59e0-8232-8633e6adcc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302248} {:answer/id #uuid "4df960ef-15bb-575e-abe6-7eb4ec4fcbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302249} {:answer/id #uuid "e737328b-6d06-5289-99e1-58f03bdeb38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302250} {:answer/id #uuid "91361bc0-3a63-5084-a396-0725cc52af54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302251} {:answer/id #uuid "73f1cd39-b5fa-56d8-9655-abd022fb838d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3697d24e-db87-59e0-8232-8633e6adcc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302248} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c7f1735-e4ea-57b4-b36c-82e173ec4951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ec38e30-b9a4-5c3b-89e7-0bc6ddab674a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160374" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,-3),(2,-2),(1,3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n- $(-2,2)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by an angle of $\\pi $ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,3),(-2,2),(-1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "698904b5-8def-56f9-808f-1f86e923f280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302255} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1156bd-ba34-57e6-82d4-076adca071a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302256} {:answer/id #uuid "efe11742-d61d-5bd2-a765-ee8df2c69bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302257} {:answer/id #uuid "001eb2af-a482-5264-bcd2-34d157942e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302258} {:answer/id #uuid "4e81369e-a07b-5f0e-8861-2431f87e1369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "001eb2af-a482-5264-bcd2-34d157942e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302258} :answer-field/id #uuid "108ada0e-2296-5b6c-9dff-d68f0bc0682b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e31cede0-51e5-5629-b2ba-9af98c0d402b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160618" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-1),(2,-3),(1,5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(-2,3)$\n- $(-1,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,1),(-2,3),(-1,-5).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f73592d-1a3a-58aa-be0b-c2bf22972c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/489bc530fc35e2e740038264f2be05bffb90214249ec5602591623d457325db3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293892} {:answer/id #uuid "b5653e6b-7125-50a8-9ac0-67659ec5449a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293893} {:answer/id #uuid "65a3beed-47ed-544c-80fd-8e3487b18804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/5bd4d8cc2643767b82acc0e61d168f5b387be67a7ff96634604e0d6828cc288b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293894} {:answer/id #uuid "daa88a91-2d37-5256-bdd5-2a7499c9b507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/efb79fc1071f79597c81a0a974e7f6b21d361ac046f728625e2fe1c02bb507eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293895} {:answer/id #uuid "2acb9fbd-6a13-5bc4-aa20-d80083518450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/2da0ffb873eb230718aab29c7dd09e015a0801b7ecb3b7dea91102f30b3e8816.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5653e6b-7125-50a8-9ac0-67659ec5449a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/ba328088ca187ea28b241a302a486da1145a51c7e134d8d4f529f5f4a916ddc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293893} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c79d7c0-dfcf-5cba-beb2-f25c807802d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75802105-915e-5223-a4a4-b4c2831bb162" :question/math-academy-id "q-160619" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/f3e31d1c15a97adea32790eafe75ad1bc9f70c854b516b98de6648ec8d7bcb87.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/ae800b271848948b5f60f5cd7c1cecd163c2aa7399e9a87e2a52d158857c6a42.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a451e005-9920-55ba-aba2-4a82df7fdc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/4a78a3e98da4c602b4e8a2ebbe8860f75e507ec2ec5c20df0e59100f96a099ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293899} {:answer/id #uuid "12c5a080-b22a-52c1-9b3c-1e3a4833e96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/cf86cad03c80018ac3976a64780650c827d7c93a0889286c711c5aa69d2b899f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293900} {:answer/id #uuid "1bef1177-6e7a-54d6-8454-e86c89efdb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f2beba76039ead47c83c93aaad5f9c4717a4abd2b803e65157f23d770f4f9181.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293901} {:answer/id #uuid "4504772e-8da4-5cff-a3c3-c6172ca608b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/3edafbd6273f8058114c606c8b2a288e52b348d560867b6ea90e8b981b486904.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293902} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6b05d3-925f-5dad-adbd-f539eb186ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/82091407834f5d7aa8caf6e01cb800a3f7b1999cb0c6e568c899d43479b1d7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a451e005-9920-55ba-aba2-4a82df7fdc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/4a78a3e98da4c602b4e8a2ebbe8860f75e507ec2ec5c20df0e59100f96a099ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293899} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c52ba30-782e-5f65-9f49-6868e4cb05f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efaa3158-b486-5ce8-9ecd-d7b2e13036d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160621" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/b75a7519c7953b7a3c5bd5b5e61e961ca26c3f0dc9e1aab32bf2c639dd95325f.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/8ea257c65769c4d6f67f55ce6533c1bfdfe45debcf1ba532b9ec3f717238507a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533fe1e0-736f-56d4-808f-c1d14b2743d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283685} {:answer/id #uuid "1dceeb35-0cb2-5a43-8e49-86d4583f28b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283686} {:answer/id #uuid "c7eb0977-8a2d-5218-b250-3fa7d83eb3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283687} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c0cc42-8a0b-5c9c-ac44-596917ab7319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283688} {:answer/id #uuid "3d16dded-e01f-512b-b59e-d410e6956f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dceeb35-0cb2-5a43-8e49-86d4583f28b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283686} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd86fb08-e9a6-50ff-89b2-48d975d8101e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37ce60a1-7ee2-5ebe-8858-c59ff464ba06" :question/math-academy-id "q-160643" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,1),(-1,0),(-1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(-2,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,0),(0,1),(-2,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b8cce3-342c-5d43-9666-d1f16ea5ef6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283692} {:answer/id #uuid "3da4c708-2c9b-5200-b8c9-1c381670eedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283693} {:answer/id #uuid "c6666819-cfa2-5225-956a-905d8b63b19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283694} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbb4d98-4041-5882-8d32-a049dad37879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283695} {:answer/id #uuid "0a85f801-e304-50a1-bbb5-53fc60815182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a85f801-e304-50a1-bbb5-53fc60815182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283696} :answer-field/id #uuid "2701afd1-8c74-50cf-8cb6-61b818707a0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f78fbb4-97dc-56c5-a843-630741ba1e9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160645" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-3,1),(1,-4),(1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(-1,3)$\n- $(4,1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\,-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-1,-3),(4,1),(0,1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e642302b-2c97-566a-bc78-c5cc15eeb8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302262} {:answer/id #uuid "abe73f35-7b0d-56f3-9aac-e923a6b1fe1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302263} {:answer/id #uuid "a8032f23-fcb4-53dc-a28d-542950709418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302264} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4fe7c8-beeb-5d20-a2b9-7a1dde933a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302265} {:answer/id #uuid "069f3ebf-61eb-519c-8cd0-87d6077ab52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4fe7c8-beeb-5d20-a2b9-7a1dde933a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302265} :answer-field/id #uuid "60f038ee-6e0a-5901-9bfb-2e937307a9b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "805b41fe-1938-5f5c-95ff-fff97c430efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160648" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(1,-1),(-2,3),(-1,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-3,-2)$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,1),(-3,-2),(0,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88da30c2-feaa-5d94-9c98-e41fd1ed9ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091104} {:answer/id #uuid "93a9126c-4c6c-5b2d-95c6-cfed9b180113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091105} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9a95e0-ae9b-5161-a560-ee947bd76206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091106} {:answer/id #uuid "02bb3447-584b-5dae-af10-da7e71bffad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091107} {:answer/id #uuid "7c486708-be44-5f4c-a56a-3f4c2807527c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93a9126c-4c6c-5b2d-95c6-cfed9b180113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/q-81450-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091105} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6cb8394-67d6-5bcb-a291-d8c3a2f43b72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be344c11-b1a1-5df9-959f-34ab811cf29b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81450" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/d111b1df75cfaa0d86d17c06584c57b19ca4711ae3e6720e68d1704fd72d86ae.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039248/assets/dd0496914b35f969896c6a54907fbfa5ea6c139c878d8682b933a8757bde7524.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "583eebc8-9b82-554d-b2de-5c335db960f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283713} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae11bac-66c8-5798-8fa5-a2f753aa3798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283714} {:answer/id #uuid "ed32353d-285a-581f-84a7-665c32f26c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283715} {:answer/id #uuid "14e4b63d-27b0-5e04-a172-a0cd24c58b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283716} {:answer/id #uuid "341b1054-3b59-571d-b0bb-be4696fb5426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "583eebc8-9b82-554d-b2de-5c335db960f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283713} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3733fa4-37ac-5b8e-8d55-f9b595ba5660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4efc51d3-b393-57b6-8514-a14b7c1d9942" :question/math-academy-id "q-81819" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,4),(-3,-3),(-1,7).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-3)$\n- $(-4,-4)$\n- $(1,-7)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-4),(3,3),(1,-7).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b5f433-bd4e-5f65-b834-ac07a468506c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/ca19bbcea3f01e99b0c606ad719686d84bca7b4dbfa1596f7b28e27fa494c44c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283720} {:answer/id #uuid "f2233722-a9fc-5e5b-8758-4ebf4e5d9785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/90dcf60e19c9031927df68a1d0be6b85c47910e6ec92e9052c292f667971d7f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283721} {:answer/id 